WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.060
Onion services are sites or services that are reached inside the Tor network using addresses that end in .onion.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:14.760
Unlike ordinary websites reached through an exit relay, an onion service does not require traffic to leave Tor for the public internet.

3
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:22.140
Both the user and the service build Tor circuits, and the connection meets at a rendezvous point inside the network.

4
00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.280
This design can protect the user's location and can also protect the service's network location.

5
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.840
A .onion address is not just a decorative domain name.

6
00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:38.620
Modern onion addresses are derived from cryptographic keys, which means the address is tied to the service's identity.

7
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:47.280
If a person carefully verifies the address from a trusted source, the address itself helps confirm that the browser is reaching the intended onion service.

8
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.900
This is why copying a .onion address from an untrusted page is risky.

9
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.400
A look-alike address or misleading link can send the user somewhere else.

10
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:00.320
Onion services have legitimate uses.

11
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:06.660
News organizations may provide onion mirrors so readers can reach reporting in places where the normal site is blocked or monitored.

12
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.400
Secure drop systems may use onion services to reduce exposure for sources.

13
00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:16.540
Communities may host services where location privacy is important.

14
00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:22.320
Internal research environments may use onion addressing to experiment with privacy-preserving access.

15
00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:28.820
The technology is neutral; responsible use depends on purpose, consent, and law.

16
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:33.740
Because onion addresses are long and unfamiliar, human error is a real risk.

17
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:41.600
A person may mistype an address, follow an outdated bookmark, or trust a link copied through an untrusted forum.

18
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:51.140
The safer pattern is to locate the onion address from the organization's normal public materials, compare it carefully, and then bookmark it once verified.

19
00:01:51.240 --> 00:02:00.120
Verification may feel tedious, but it is the difference between using onion identity as a protection and treating a random string as if it were proof.

20
00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:04.780
For important services, verification should happen before the moment of urgency.

21
00:02:05.060 --> 00:02:08.740
Onion services also change the HTTPS conversation.

22
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:15.480
Since the onion address is tied to cryptographic identity, the connection has a built-in service authentication property.

23
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:23.180
Some onion sites still use HTTPS, especially for browser interface consistency or organizational policy.

24
00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.600
Others rely on the onion service design.

25
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:35.060
The key idea is that onion services remove the ordinary Tor exit relay from the path, so the exit-node risk described earlier does not apply in the same way.

26
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:39.020
This does not mean every onion service is safe or trustworthy.

27
00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.720
A phishing page can exist at a .onion address.

28
00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.980
A service can mishandle data.

29
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:52.280
A user can still disclose personal information, download unsafe files, or log into an account that identifies them.

30
00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:58.540
The privacy of the route does not prove the ethics, quality, or security of the destination.

31
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:02.640
Users still need to evaluate the source, purpose, and content.

32
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:07.540
Network privacy and destination trust remain separate judgments.

33
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:11.340
The practical habit for onion services is verification.

34
00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.440
Use onion addresses published by the organization through trusted channels.

35
00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:18.980
Bookmark important addresses rather than re-searching for them.

36
00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:21.960
Be cautious with copied links.

37
00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:29.420
Understand that an onion service can improve network privacy while leaving normal website judgment fully in place.

38
00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:36.400
In short, .onion changes where the connection goes and who can observe the path; it does not remove the need to think.

39
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:40.420
This habit reduces rushed and avoidable high-stakes mistakes.
